1
00:00:00,445 --> 00:00:02,415
Here we have a beaker.

2
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:03,597
Some clear liquid.

3
00:00:03,597 --> 00:00:06,355
A magnetic stirrer going round and round.

4
00:00:06,355 --> 00:00:07,537
Nothing particularly exciting.

5
00:00:07,537 --> 00:00:10,689
Except this solution contains thirty grams of silver.

6
00:00:10,689 --> 00:00:15,023
You cannot see it because the silver is dissolved as ions.

7
00:00:15,023 --> 00:00:16,599
But it is there.

8
00:00:16,599 --> 00:00:19,357
And I would quite like it back.

9
00:00:20,441 --> 00:00:24,219
We are working with a virtual silver nitrate solution.

10
00:00:24,219 --> 00:00:27,997
Thirty grams of silver, in one kilogram of water.

11
00:00:27,997 --> 00:00:32,195
What could turn those dissolved silver ions into a solid?

12
00:00:32,195 --> 00:00:36,812
We could add water, add sodium chloride, or simply stir faster.

13
00:00:36,812 --> 00:00:38,072
Take your pick.

14
00:00:38,072 --> 00:00:42,689
The stirrer is enthusiastic, but enthusiasm is not a separation method.

15
00:00:43,938 --> 00:00:46,215
Let us add sodium chloride.

16
00:00:46,215 --> 00:00:48,492
Ordinary salt, dissolved in water.

17
00:00:48,492 --> 00:00:50,769
Watch the white cloud form.

18
00:00:50,769 --> 00:00:54,868
Silver ions meet chloride ions, and silver chloride appears.

19
00:00:54,868 --> 00:00:57,145
The silver has not vanished.

20
00:00:57,145 --> 00:01:02,610
We have changed where it resides: from dissolved species into a solid.

21
00:01:04,012 --> 00:01:06,000
So what actually happened?

22
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,956
Silver chloride can dissolve, and silver and chloride ions can form silver chloride again.

23
00:01:12,956 --> 00:01:15,938
At equilibrium, these opposing processes balance.

24
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:21,901
When their ion activity product exceeds the solubility product, precipitation is favored.

25
00:01:21,901 --> 00:01:25,876
The solution adjusts until that condition is satisfied.

26
00:01:27,195 --> 00:01:30,409
This curve is calculated from the equilibrium database.

27
00:01:30,409 --> 00:01:34,024
The horizontal axis shows how much chloride we add.

28
00:01:34,024 --> 00:01:37,639
The vertical axis shows how much silver remains dissolved.

29
00:01:37,639 --> 00:01:42,058
Notice the sharp fall near one chloride ion per silver ion.

30
00:01:42,058 --> 00:01:43,665
The last traces matter.

31
00:01:43,665 --> 00:01:46,879
A clear liquid is not a chemical analysis.

32
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:51,699
Move the dose to recover at least ninety nine point nine percent.

33
00:01:53,056 --> 00:01:56,137
Now we separate the solid from the liquid.

34
00:01:56,137 --> 00:01:58,447
The white material is silver chloride.

35
00:01:58,447 --> 00:02:06,148
It contains our silver, but it also contains chlorine, so the solid weighs more than the silver we started with.

36
00:02:06,148 --> 00:02:07,688
Filtration collects the precipitate.

37
00:02:07,688 --> 00:02:10,768
It does not turn it into a metal.

38
00:02:12,029 --> 00:02:17,808
But hey, silver chloride is not exactly something you bring to the pawn shop.

39
00:02:17,808 --> 00:02:21,111
It is much better to bring metallic silver.

40
00:02:21,111 --> 00:02:23,175
You know: oxidation state zero.

41
00:02:23,175 --> 00:02:25,651
So we need more than precipitation.

42
00:02:25,651 --> 00:02:26,890
We need reduction.

43
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:28,541
Silver must gain electrons.

44
00:02:29,768 --> 00:02:35,196
Let us rewind our virtual experiment to the original silver nitrate solution.

45
00:02:35,196 --> 00:02:39,266
The white chloride precipitate belongs to our first route.

46
00:02:39,266 --> 00:02:44,694
For this second route, we deliberately use a chloride free starting solution.

47
00:02:44,694 --> 00:02:47,860
That distinction matters: chloride changes the equilibria.

48
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:53,288
We are comparing two recovery methods, not making the chloride mysteriously disappear.

49
00:02:54,743 --> 00:03:00,941
This is a Pourbaix diagram: a map of equilibrium phases and dissolved species.

50
00:03:00,941 --> 00:03:03,325
Left to right is pH.

51
00:03:03,325 --> 00:03:09,046
Up and down is electrode potential, measured against the standard hydrogen electrode.

52
00:03:09,046 --> 00:03:12,383
Moving upward generally favors more oxidized states.

53
00:03:12,383 --> 00:03:14,767
Moving downward favors reduced states.

54
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:19,535
The dashed lines mark the equilibrium stability limits of water.

55
00:03:20,743 --> 00:03:24,734
At our starting point, silver is dissolved as silver ions.

56
00:03:24,734 --> 00:03:26,729
Watch the marker move down.

57
00:03:26,729 --> 00:03:30,720
Cross into the metallic silver region, and the beaker changes.

58
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:33,514
Move upward again, and the metal dissolves.

59
00:03:33,514 --> 00:03:35,909
This is an imposed potential demonstration.

60
00:03:35,909 --> 00:03:38,702
Clicking the map supplies no real reagent.

61
00:03:38,702 --> 00:03:45,487
In game mode, find a point where at least ninety nine percent of the silver is metallic.

62
00:03:46,802 --> 00:03:48,127
Now move sideways.

63
00:03:48,127 --> 00:03:52,100
At sufficiently alkaline conditions, silver oxide can become stable.

64
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:56,074
That is a different solid, and a different route.

65
00:03:56,074 --> 00:04:00,489
A pH change is not the same as adding electrons.

66
00:04:00,489 --> 00:04:04,021
The diagram helps us tell these changes apart.

67
00:04:04,021 --> 00:04:10,202
Its boundaries depend on the total concentration and the species included in the calculation.

68
00:04:11,565 --> 00:04:12,919
Now compare copper.

69
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:20,591
These two reference lines show the metal and dissolved ion couples at explicitly stated free ion activities.

70
00:04:20,591 --> 00:04:23,299
Copper has the lower reduction potential.

71
00:04:23,299 --> 00:04:28,714
Between the lines, silver reduction and copper oxidation can be favorable together.

72
00:04:28,714 --> 00:04:31,873
The vertical separation suggests a redox pair.

73
00:04:31,873 --> 00:04:36,837
It is a thermodynamic clue, not a prediction of reaction speed.

74
00:04:38,059 --> 00:04:40,117
We need an electron donor.

75
00:04:40,117 --> 00:04:44,232
Would you choose copper metal, sodium chloride, or silver metal?

76
00:04:44,232 --> 00:04:47,524
Copper can lose electrons as it enters solution.

77
00:04:47,524 --> 00:04:51,638
Silver ions can accept those electrons and become silver metal.

78
00:04:51,638 --> 00:04:57,811
We do not need an external power supply for this pair under the selected conditions.

79
00:04:59,119 --> 00:05:03,089
In goes a measured portion of brownish copper powder.

80
00:05:03,089 --> 00:05:06,617
At the copper surface, copper atoms release electrons.

81
00:05:06,617 --> 00:05:10,586
Silver ions take them up, and metallic silver forms.

82
00:05:10,586 --> 00:05:14,115
The liquid becomes blue as copper ions accumulate.

83
00:05:14,115 --> 00:05:17,202
We are speeding up the visual sequence.

84
00:05:17,202 --> 00:05:22,495
This animation illustrates the transformation; it is not a calculated reaction rate.

85
00:05:23,972 --> 00:05:29,127
One copper atom becomes a copper two plus ion and releases two electrons.

86
00:05:29,127 --> 00:05:31,903
Two silver ions each accept one electron.

87
00:05:31,903 --> 00:05:35,076
Add the half reactions, and the electrons cancel.

88
00:05:35,076 --> 00:05:40,231
That is why one mole of copper can recover two moles of silver.

89
00:05:40,231 --> 00:05:48,956
Our thirty grams of silver correspond to about eight point eight four grams of copper consumed in this ideal two couple model.

90
00:05:50,233 --> 00:05:52,468
There is our metallic silver.

91
00:05:52,468 --> 00:05:53,362
Collect it.

92
00:05:53,362 --> 00:06:01,853
The ideal model predicts nearly thirty grams, but a real product still needs separation, washing, and a purity check.

93
00:06:01,853 --> 00:06:04,982
Excess copper could contaminate the recovered material.

94
00:06:04,982 --> 00:06:10,345
A favorable reaction does not automatically produce a pawn shop ready product.

95
00:06:14,508 --> 00:06:17,476
Now separate the reactions into two beakers.

96
00:06:17,476 --> 00:06:22,988
This new demonstration uses zero point zero one molal copper and silver solutions.

97
00:06:22,988 --> 00:06:25,108
Copper oxidizes at the anode.

98
00:06:25,108 --> 00:06:28,924
Electrons travel through the wire to the silver cathode.

99
00:06:28,924 --> 00:06:32,740
The salt bridge carries ions to maintain charge balance.

100
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:36,132
The voltmeter uses the calculated free ion activities.

101
00:06:36,132 --> 00:06:39,100
The chemistry has not changed its logic.

102
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,492
We have given the electrons an external path.

103
00:06:41,086 --> 00:06:45,066
Open the circuit and the external electron flow stops.

104
00:06:45,066 --> 00:06:48,162
Close it and the spontaneous direction returns.

105
00:06:48,162 --> 00:06:56,122
An opposing power supply can reverse the selected reaction if its voltage is sufficient in this ideal model.

106
00:06:56,122 --> 00:07:00,102
Real cells also have resistance, overpotentials, and competing reactions.

107
00:07:00,102 --> 00:07:08,062
In game mode, this is the actual solution bench: try the circuit controls and inspect the Nernst calculation.

108
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:11,270
Two routes.

109
00:07:11,270 --> 00:07:14,790
The first changed solubility and collected silver chloride.

110
00:07:14,790 --> 00:07:18,750
The second changed oxidation state and produced silver metal.

111
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:20,950
The beaker was never empty.

112
00:07:20,950 --> 00:07:25,350
We just learned how to make its contents reveal themselves.

113
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:27,990
The answer lies in chemical equilibrium.

114
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:32,830
And the person who understands it becomes very difficult to surprise.

115
00:07:33,128 --> 00:07:34,994
Try the lesson yourself.

116
00:07:34,994 --> 00:07:36,859
Change the chloride dose.

117
00:07:36,859 --> 00:07:38,725
Explore the silver diagram.

118
00:07:38,725 --> 00:07:40,590
Compare copper and silver.

119
00:07:40,590 --> 00:07:46,187
Then ask the next question: what happens when other metals are present?

120
00:07:46,187 --> 00:07:48,518
That is where selectivity begins.

121
00:07:48,518 --> 00:07:50,384
Welcome to Bergslagen Chemisol.
